class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = new Calculator();
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));
        System.out.println(c.add(10.5, 20.5));
    }
}

explaination:-
This program demonstrates method overloading in Java.
I created a class called `Calculator` with three methods having the same name, `add()`, but different parameter lists.
The first method accepts two integers, the second accepts three integers, and the third accepts two double values.
When I call the `add()` method with different arguments, Java selects the appropriate method based on the parameter list. This is called **compile-time polymorphism**, because the method call is resolved by the compiler.
Method overloading improves code readability and allows us to use the same method name for similar operations.
